-- examples/checks/agg-incr-stockholes.rofl — agg_incr_stockholes: THE HOLES OF A
-- WORLD WITH NO AGGREGATE, under RETRACTION. A hole is a base, frozen row, which
-- clearing the derived layer keeps; the stock evaluator of the TypeScript engine
-- wrote its holes without noting them, so a world evaluated again after a
-- retraction (or an excision) kept the hole of a fault the world no longer has,
-- and `hr_any` went on holding while the Rust engine, which drops them, did not.
-- The state must be the one of a world without the fact.
--
--   hr_k(b, oops)   1 + oops is no number: the rule is holed, and so is what negates it
edb(hr_k).
hr_k(a, 1).
hr_k(b, oops).
hr_x(C, Y) :- hr_k(C, K), Y is K + 1.
hr_n(C) :- hr_k(C, _), not hr_x(C, _).
hr_any(1) :- hole[$kernel]($rule(_), arith_type_error).
hr_withdrawn(1) :- hole[$kernel]($rule(_), support_withdrawn).

-- the state after the retraction, row by row
-- expect-row: hr_k[main](a,1) tick base support=0
-- expect-row: hr_x[main](a,2) tick drv support=1
-- and nothing of what the fact supported before
-- expect-no-row: hr_any[main](1) tick drv support=1
-- expect-no-row: hr_withdrawn[main](1) tick drv support=1
-- expect-no-row: hr_k[main](b,oops) tick base support=0
